replace('A \\emph{direct-reading guard} avoids assuming archive membership. More than $f$ queried lower bounds above the reference guarantee at least one clean exceedance. For $f=0$, non-exceedance requires every station\'s upper bound at or below the reference. With arbitrary corruption and no physical upper bounds, safe decisions are generally unidentifiable, so it abstains. Sequential and full-network guards give identical decisions on matched responses.',
        r'''A \emph{direct-reading guard} certifies only what the new measurements themselves justify. If a queried reading is $y_v$, its clean lower and upper bounds are $y_v-\eta_v$ and $y_v+\eta_v$, where $\eta_v$ is the declared error limit. More than $f$ lower bounds above the reference guarantee that at least one high reading is clean.

For $f=0$, non-exceedance requires querying every station and finding every upper bound at or below the reference. With arbitrary corruption and no physical upper bounds, a faulty response could conceal high pollution, so the guard abstains from non-exceedance. Sequential and full-network guards give identical decisions on matched responses. The sequential guard saves measurements by stopping once it has a certificate; its validity still depends on the reading-error and fault assumptions.''')
replace('With one corruption, only 20.53\\% of trials receive certificates. Box-plan prioritization uses 41/15 more queries than training-frequency order. Validation-selected ordering improves by only 0.219\\%/0.080\\%, with losses in 7/4 of eighteen roles. This supports fewer queries than full measurement, not superiority to simple orders.',
        'The guard issued no wrong certificates in the tested trials and saved 17.24\\% of measurements without corruption. With one corruption, however, only 20.53\\% of trials receive certificates. Savings include trials ending in abstention.\n\nA simple ordering is also competitive: querying by training exceedance frequency uses 41 fewer queries without corruption and 15 fewer with one corruption than box-plan prioritization. Choosing an order on validation data improves on that simple order by only 0.219\\% and 0.080\\%, with losses in seven and four of eighteen roles respectively. The evidence supports savings against measuring every station, but does not establish an advantage over simple query orders.')
replace('Across 23 fitted roles, 1,032 subset/tolerance checks agree with rank calculations. All full comparison matrices have zero numerical kernel; separate nonzero-kernel and singular controls exercise other cases. Exact modular checks certify rank for 23 operators and 246 endpoint blocks. Independent calculations confirm 414 fixed configurations, 122 stability frontiers/594 budget points, 76 adaptive optima/72,950 subproblems, and 239 boundary counterexamples.',
        'These checks ask whether the implementations follow the mathematical rules, rather than whether the rules describe every real outage. Across 23 fitted roles, 1,032 outage-subset and tolerance checks agree with direct matrix-rank calculations. A matrix\'s kernel is the set of vectors it maps to zero. All full comparison matrices have zero numerical kernel; separate controls test matrices with nonzero kernels or singular endpoint maps. Exact arithmetic modulo primes certifies rank for the 23 operators and 246 endpoint blocks.\n\nIndependent calculations also confirm all 414 fixed repair configurations. They verify 122 stability frontiers containing 594 budget points, 76 adaptive optima covering 72,950 subproblems, and 239 counterexamples at the separation boundary. These are dependent computational checks, not independent field trials.')
replace('Of 272 joint cases, 260 are feasible and 12 inconsistent: 155 below-reference certificates and 117 abstentions, including those 12. All 40 truly positive cases abstain; none establishes an above-reference joint certificate. Two planted joint-only positive controls check that capability separately. A separate scalar check has 216 below-reference, 44 uncertain and 12 inconsistent cases (Figure~\\ref{fig:math-integration}); it is not the joint result. The bound/search checks assure computation under nominal coefficients, not physical model validity.',
        'The joint verifier\'s most important limitation is its failure to certify any above-reference decision in the tested hidden-target cases. Of 272 cases, 260 have feasible models and 12 are inconsistent. It issues 155 below-reference certificates and abstains in 117, including the inconsistent cases. All 40 truly positive cases receive abstentions. Two planted examples separately check that the algorithm can certify a joint exceedance when the model permits it.\n\nA scalar check, involving one score rather than the joint maximum, gives 216 below-reference decisions, 44 uncertain cases and 12 inconsistent cases (Figure~\\ref{fig:math-integration}). This is a different endpoint. The bound and search checks support computation with the stated coefficients; they do not validate physical error limits.')
replace('Earlier protocols are separate: historical snapshot gain is median 3.1\\% against dense Gaussian conditioning (7/8 numerical gains, 2/8 within-network Holm comparisons); retrospective first-day gain is median 34.0\\% against an implemented Kalman/VAR smoother (nine configurations, 5/9 Holm comparisons). Separate Delhi causal gain is 23.2\\% on 399 station-days/58 dates; later same-network PM-only gain is 9.5\\% on 74/19. Beijing has 5.93\\% higher RMSE on 502 cases; historical Delhi GRIN seeds all beat sheaf. Weather controls establish no improvement. None replaces the primary benchmark or supplies independent replication.',
        'Earlier studies provide context but use different datasets and information rules, so their gains should not be pooled with the primary benchmark. A historical snapshot study has median gain 3.1\\% against dense Gaussian conditioning: seven of eight comparisons favour sheaf numerically, but only two pass a within-network Holm correction for multiple comparisons. A retrospective first-day study has median gain 34.0\\% against an implemented Kalman/VAR smoother across nine configurations; five pass Holm correction.\n\nA separate Delhi causal cohort gives 23.2\\% gain on 399 station-days across 58 dates. A later PM-only period from the same network gives 9.5\\% on 74 station-days across 19 dates. There are negative findings too: Beijing has 5.93\\% higher RMSE than VAR on 502 cases, and historical Delhi GRIN seeds all beat sheaf. Weather controls establish no improvement. These studies do not replace the primary benchmark or provide independent replications.')
replace('At illustrative hourly PM2.5 reference 60 $\\mu$g/m$^3$, Indian calibrated alerts have 69.1\\% sensitivity/9.6\\% false positives versus VAR 52.7\\%/6.1\\%, on 1,177 positive/4,012 negative hours. This is a tradeoff, not an equal-false-alarm win. Natural-mask transplantation gives RMSE 19.65 versus city mean 20.46 $\\mu$g/m$^3$ (3.94\\% gain; 719 cells/155 patterns); the US counterpart has eight cells. Across 190 Indian daily proxy cases, sheaf/city tie at 73.7\\% and all methods miss the sole Poor-or-worse day. Four bulletin matches are descriptive \\citep{cpcbrecent,caqm2025}. Original Indian/US joint trials give no definite decision, UK four model-conditional below-reference decisions; these precede and differ from the 272-case audit. No regulatory decision recovery, physical intervention or health benefit was measured.',
        'The environmental evidence supports computational monitoring, with limited alert performance. At an illustrative hourly PM2.5 reference of 60 $\\mu$g/m$^3$, sensitivity is the fraction of truly above-reference hours flagged, and the false-positive rate is the fraction of below-reference hours incorrectly flagged. Indian calibrated sheaf alerts give 69.1\\% sensitivity and 9.6\\% false positives, versus VAR\'s 52.7\\% and 6.1\\%, on 1,177 positive and 4,012 negative hours. Sheaf finds more positives while raising more false alarms; this does not establish an improvement at equal false-alarm rates.\n\nApplying naturally occurring missing-data patterns to recorded Indian values gives PM2.5 RMSE 19.65 versus city mean 20.46 $\\mu$g/m$^3$, a 3.94\\% gain on 719 hidden cells and 155 patterns. The US counterpart has only eight cells. In 190 Indian daily proxy cases, sheaf and city mean tie at 73.7\\%, and every method misses the sole day categorized Poor or worse. Four matches to official bulletins are descriptive \\citep{cpcbrecent,caqm2025}.\n\nThe original Indian and US joint trials yield no definite decision; a separate UK trial gives four model-conditional below-reference decisions. These precede and differ from the 272-case audit. No deployed regulatory decision recovery, physical intervention or health benefit was measured.')
replace('It can help keep estimates and decision checks available during outages.',
        'The tests demonstrate reconstruction of artificially hidden readings and decision checks within declared models. Keeping estimates and checks available during real outages remains the intended application.')
replace('We thank OpenAQ, EPA, DEFRA AURN, the NSW Government and CPCB for data,',
        'We thank OpenAQ, EPA, DEFRA AURN, the NSW Government and the Central Pollution Control Board (CPCB) for data,')

for pattern in [r'\\begin\{(?:proposition|theorem)\}.*?\\end\{(?:proposition|theorem)\}',
                r'\\textbf\{Proof\.\}.*?\\textit\{QED\.\}',
                r'\\begin\{figure\}.*?\\end\{figure\}']:
    assert re.findall(pattern, s, re.S) == re.findall(pattern, old, re.S), pattern
